SDK threading: block multi-object waits on a condition variable instead of polling (CR-09).
Phase B item B4 (docs/sdk-from-source.md, docs/weak-mac-perf-20261008.md step 5). Applied by
tools/build_sdk_from_source.py (SDK_BEHAVIOUR_PATCHES).
PosixConditionBase::WaitMultiple try-locked every handle, spun on yield() while any was
held, and otherwise slept min(remaining, 1 ms) with the remaining time truncated to whole
milliseconds. Alertable waits call it in 1 ms slices, so the remaining time was always
< 1 ms -> sleep_for(0): a waiting thread busy-spun for the whole wait. The audio worker
(WaitAny on its client semaphores, alertable, 500 ms) burned ~74-83 ms of CPU per 100 ms.
Now the waiter registers a wait block on each of its handles, locks the distinct handles in
address order, checks, and blocks on the block's condition variable until one of its
handles is signalled (every Signal/Release/thread-exit path calls NotifyMultiWaiters under
the handle's mutex) or the exact deadline. Any/all semantics, post_execution, result
indices and the alertable (APC) slicing are unchanged. The audio worker's extra 500 ms
sleep after an idle WaitAny is dropped. Cvar wait_multiple_polling (default false)
restores the old behaviour of both for A/B tests.

